{"conjecture": "Long-running systems degrade not because individual changes are wrong, but because each locally correct change implicitly encodes assumptions about the system's current structure—assumptions that become false premises for future changes. Over time, the gap between the code's actual behavior and the developers' working model of that behavior widens, so every new change requires reconstructing an ever-larger invisible context that no single change introduced but every change helped obscure. The cost of change grows asymptotically toward the cost of rediscovering the system's design from scratch."}
